            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The fluvial drainage network of India is an intricate geomorphic tapestry shaped by tectonic collisions, lithological resistance, structural faults, and climatic precipitation regimes.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Genetic Classification of Drainage Systems</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Antecedent (Insequent) Drainage:</strong> Rivers that existed <em>before</em> the uplift of the Himalayan mountains. As tectonic folding raised the mountain ridges across their paths, the rivers maintained their original channels by downcutting at a rate equal to or exceeding tectonic uplift, carving vertical, razor-sharp <strong>transverse gorges</strong> thousands of meters deep. Classic examples: <strong>Indus</strong>, <strong>Satluj</strong>, <strong>Ganga (Bhagirathi)</strong>, <strong>Brahmaputra (Dihang)</strong>, <strong>Kosi</strong>, and <strong>Subansiri</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Superimposed (Epigenetic) Drainage:</strong> Rivers that initially developed on a younger overlying sedimentary layer or volcanic sheet. Over time, as erosion stripped away the upper layer, the river incised its inherited drainage geometry directly into the underlying, ancient crystalline discordant basement. Classic examples: <strong>Subarnarekha</strong>, <strong>Damodar</strong>, <strong>Chambal</strong>, and <strong>Banas</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Consequent Drainage:</strong> Rivers whose flow direction is directly determined by the initial regional slope of the land. Most Peninsular east-flowing rivers (Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) are consequent upon the eastward tilt of the Peninsular shield.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Geometric Drainage Patterns</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Morphological Description</th><th>Prominent Indian Examples</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Dendritic</strong></td><td>Tree-like branching where tributaries join the main stream at acute angles; develops in uniform lithology.</td><td>Indo-Gangetic Plain, Godavari, Mahanadi, Deccan basalt plateau.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Trellis</strong></td><td>Sub-parallel primary streams joined at right angles by short transverse tributaries; develops along alternating hard/soft folded strata.</td><td>Folded Siwaliks, Lesser Himalayas, Nallamala Hills.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Radial</strong></td><td>Streams radiate outward in all directions from a central highland dome or volcano.</td><td><strong>Amarkantak Plateau</strong> (Narmada to west, Son to north, Mahanadi headwaters to south); <strong>Girnar Hills</strong>.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Centripetal</strong></td><td>Streams converge from all surrounding higher ground into a central depression or sink.</td><td><strong>Loktak Lake</strong> (Manipur), <strong>Sambhar Lake</strong> (Rajasthan).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Rectangular</strong></td><td>Right-angled river bends governed by intersecting joint and fault systems in resistant rocks.</td><td>Vindhyan scarplands, streams of Chambal and Ken valleys.</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        ",
