            "id": "m02",
            "title": "Master Drainage Divides of the Indian Subcontinent",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The hydrography of the Indian subcontinent is bifurcated into two unequal maritime drainage realms—the <strong>Bay of Bengal Drainage</strong> and the <strong>Arabian Sea Drainage</strong>—separated by an unbroken continental line of elevation known as the <strong>Great Water Divide of India</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Alignment of the Great Continental Water Divide</h4>\n            <p>Traceable as a continuous line on the topographic map, the Great Water Divide runs:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li>From the <strong>Kailash-Mansarovar crest</strong> and the high Himalayas in the north;</li>\n                <li>Down through the <strong>Ambala Swell</strong> and the <strong>Delhi-Aravalli Ridge</strong>, which severs the Indus basin (flowing to Arabian Sea) from the Ganga basin (Bay of Bengal);</li>\n                <li>Across eastern Rajasthan into Madhya Pradesh along the <strong>Vindhyan Range</strong>;</li>\n                <li>Southward across the <strong>Satpura Range</strong>;</li>\n                <li>Continuing along the entire $1,600\text{ km}$ crestline of the <strong>Western Ghats (Sahyadris)</strong> down to Cape Comorin (Kanyakumari).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Drainage Basin Distribution: Bay of Bengal vs Arabian Sea</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Bay of Bengal Drainage</th><th>Arabian Sea Drainage</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Percentage of Runoff</strong></td><td><strong>~77%</strong> of total Indian river discharge.</td><td><strong>~23%</strong> of total Indian river discharge.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Major River Basins</strong></td><td>Ganga, Brahmaputra, Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri, Pennar, Subarnarekha.</td><td>Indus, Narmada, Tapti, Sabarmati, Mahi, Sharavati, Mandovi, Zuari, Periyar, Bharatapuzha.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Delta Formation</strong></td><td>Extensive, multi-lobed, sediment-rich <strong>Deltas</strong>.</td><td>Deltas absent; rivers terminate in <strong>Estuaries</strong>.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Topographic Gradient</strong></td><td>Long courses across gentle, graded slopes.</td><td>Short, torrential courses down steep Western Ghats scarps (except Narmada/Tapti rifts).</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>3. Secondary Hydrographic Divides</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Delhi Ridge:</strong> The northernmost rocky spur of the Aravalli range in Delhi, dividing the Yamuna (Ganga basin) from the Ghaggar/Indus basin.</li>\n                <li><strong>Satpura Range:</strong> Forms the watershed between the westward-flowing Narmada to the north and the Tapti to the south, while simultaneously separating both from the eastward-flowing Godavari headstreams.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
