            "title": "The Indus River System: Master Stream & Trans-Himalayan Tributaries",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Indus River System</strong> is one of the world's premier trans-Himalayan fluvial networks. Extending over a total course of <strong>$2,880\text{ km}$</strong> (of which $1,114\text{ km}$ lies within India), it drains an immense basin of over $11.65\text{ lakh sq km}$ (about $3.21\text{ lakh sq km}$ in India).</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Source & Course through Ladakh</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Glacial Source:</strong> Rises in southwestern Tibet near Mount Kailash and Lake Mansarovar from a spring at <strong>Bokhar Chu (elevation ~4,164 m)</strong> in the northern slopes of the Kailash Range. In Tibet, it is reverently called <strong>Singi Khamban</strong> (meaning <em>'Lion's Mouth'</em>).</li>\n                <li><strong>Structural Trench:</strong> Enters India in Ladakh at <strong>Demchok</strong>. It flows in a straight northwestward structural depression, tightly wedged between the <strong>Ladakh Range to its north</strong> and the <strong>Zaskar Range to its south</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Leh & Zanskar Confluence:</strong> Passes through Leh, where it receives the swift, sediment-laden <strong>Zanskar River</strong> on its left bank near Nimmu (famous for the confluence of the muddy Zanskar and clear Indus, and winter <em>Chadar Trek</em>).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Trans-Himalayan Mountain Tributaries</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Left-Bank Tributaries:</strong> <strong>Zanskar</strong>, <strong>Suru</strong> (flows through Kargil), and <strong>Dras</strong> (originates near Zoji La; Dras is India's coldest inhabited town).</li>\n                <li><strong>Right-Bank Tributaries:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Shyok River (meaning 'River of Death' in Yarkandi):</strong> Originates from the <strong>Rimo Glacier</strong> in the Karakoram. Joined in the Nubra valley by the <strong>Nubra River</strong> (fed by Siachen Glacier snout).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Shigar River:</strong> Drains the massive Baltoro Glacier and joins the Indus at Skardu (Baltistan).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Gilgit River:</strong> Joins the Indus near Bunji; carries the waters of the <strong>Hunza</strong> and Gupis rivers.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Great Transverse Gorge of Nanga Parbat</h4>\n            <p>Near Bunji, at the northwestern knee of the Himalayas, the Indus makes a sharp hairpin syntaxial bend around <strong>Nanga Parbat (8,126 m)</strong>, carving a breathtaking <strong>$5,200\text{ meter deep canyon}$</strong>—one of the deepest river canyons in the world—before cutting south into Kohistan (Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, Pakistan).</p>\n        ",
