            "title": "The Ganga Headwaters & The Sacred Panch Prayag",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Ganga River</strong>—the cultural, spiritual, and agricultural artery of northern India—originates in the high Garhwal Himalayas of Uttarakhand through a complex network of glaciated headstreams converging at sacred confluences known as the <strong>Panch Prayag</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Two Primary Master Headstreams</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>The Bhagirathi:</strong> Originates at <strong>Gaumukh (3,920 m)</strong>, the terminus of the 30 km long <strong>Gangotri Glacier</strong> in Uttarkashi district. Joined by torrential mountain tributaries including the <em>Jadh Ganga (Jahnavi)</em>, <em>Kedar Ganga</em>, and the <strong>Bhilangana</strong> (at the site of the colossal <strong>Tehri Dam</strong>). Historically and spiritually, the Bhagirathi is venerated as the true Ganga.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Alaknanda:</strong> Rises from the twin <strong>Satopanth and Bhagirath Kharak Glaciers</strong> near Mana peak above the holy shrine of Badrinath. In terms of channel width and hydrological discharge, the Alaknanda carries nearly twice the volume of the Bhagirathi.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Sacred Panch Prayag (Five Confluences)</h4>\n            <p>As the Alaknanda descends southward through Chamoli, Rudraprayag, and Tehri Garhwal, it absorbs major glacier-fed tributaries at five sacred prayags (confluences):</p>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Prayag (Confluence)</th><th>Tributary River Joining</th><th>Source of Tributary</th><th>Key Sites / Notes</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>1. Vishnuprayag</strong></td>\n                        <td><strong>Dhauliganga</strong> meets Alaknanda</td>\n                        <td>Niti Pass / Kamet Glacier</td>\n                        <td>Near Joshimath; Site of 2021 Rishiganga flash flood.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>2. Nandaprayag</strong></td>\n                        <td><strong>Nandakini</strong> meets Alaknanda</td>\n                        <td>Nanda Ghunti Glacier</td>\n                        <td>Below Joshimath in Chamoli district.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>3. Karnaprayag</strong></td>\n                        <td><strong>Pindar River</strong> meets Alaknanda</td>\n                        <td>Pindari Glacier (Bageshwar)</td>\n                        <td>Ancient pilgrimage town named after Karna.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>4. Rudraprayag</strong></td>\n                        <td><strong>Mandakini (Kali Ganga)</strong> meets Alaknanda</td>\n                        <td>Chorabari Glacier (Kedarnath)</td>\n                        <td>Site of Kedarnath pilgrim route; heavily impacted in 2013.</td>\n                    </tr>\n                    <tr>\n                        <td><strong>5. Devprayag</strong></td>\n                        <td><strong>Bhagirathi</strong> meets <strong>Alaknanda</strong></td>\n                        <td>Gaumukh (Bhagirathi)</td>\n                        <td><strong>The river is formally christened the 'GANGA'.</strong></td>\n                    </tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>3. Descent into the Plains</h4>\n            <p>From Devprayag, the mighty <strong>Ganga</strong> flows through steep transverse gorges past <strong>Rishikesh</strong>, breaking out of the Shiwalik foothills into the flat aggradational Indo-Gangetic Plains at the holy city of <strong>Haridwar</strong>.</p>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Ganga is formed by the union of two master Himalayan streams: the Bhagirathi and the Alaknanda at Devprayag.",
                "Bhagirathi rises from Gaumukh (snout of Gangotri glacier in Uttarkashi); Alaknanda rises from the Satopanth and Bhagirath Kharak glaciers above Badrinath.",
                "Hydrologically, the Alaknanda is larger in discharge than the Bhagirathi, but the Bhagirathi is culturally revered as the primary source.",
                "The Panch Prayag are the 5 sacred confluences along the Alaknanda: Vishnuprayag, Nandaprayag, Karnaprayag, Rudraprayag, and Devprayag."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "Confluences in order descending downstream: 1. Vishnuprayag (Alaknanda + Dhauliganga), 2. Nandaprayag (Alaknanda + Nandakini), 3. Karnaprayag (Alaknanda + Pindar River), 4. Rudraprayag (Alaknanda + Mandakini), 5. Devprayag (Alaknanda + Bhagirathi). Below Devprayag, the combined stream is officially named 'Ganga'. The river exits the Himalayas at Rishikesh/Haridwar."
            ],
