            "title": "The Ganga: Major Right-Bank Tributaries & Peninsular Drainage Influx",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>While the Himalayan left-bank tributaries provide perennial snowmelt, the <strong>right bank of the Ganga</strong> receives the immense waters of the <strong>Yamuna</strong> and an extensive system of <strong>peninsular rivers</strong> that drain northward off the Central Highlands and Chotanagpur Plateau.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. The Son River System</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Origin:</strong> Rises on the <strong>Amarkantak Plateau</strong> in Anuppur district, MP, just east of the Narmada source.</li>\n                <li><strong>Course:</strong> Parallels the Kaimur scarp through Madhya Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh (Sonbhadra), and Bihar, meeting the Ganga on its right bank near <strong>Maner / Danapur (Patna)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Key Tributaries:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Rihand River:</strong> Rises in Chhattisgarh; site of the <strong>Govind Ballabh Pant Sagar (Rihand Dam)</strong> in Sonbhadra, UP—India's largest artificial reservoir by storage volume.</li>\n                        <li><strong>North Koel:</strong> Rises on the Ranchi plateau, flowing through Palamu Tiger Reserve to join the Son.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Gopad and Kanhar:</strong> Draining the Baghelkhand upland.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Other Right-Bank Peninsular Tributaries</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Tons (Tamas) River:</strong> Rises in the Kaimur Hills (Satna, MP); drops over the <em>Chachai / Keoti Falls</em>, joining the Ganga near Sirsa (Prayagraj).</li>\n                <li><strong>Punpun River:</strong> Rises on the Chotanagpur plateau (Palamu); flows northeast through Gaya and Patna districts, joining the Ganga at <strong>Fatuha</strong>. Considered sacred in Pinda Daan rituals.</li>\n                <li><strong>Falgu River:</strong> Formed by the union of the <em>Lilajan (Niranjana)</em> and <em>Mohana</em> at Bodh Gaya, where Lord Buddha attained Enlightenment under the Mahabodhi Tree. Famous for subterranean flow during dry months and the recently constructed <strong>Gayaji Dam</strong> (India's largest rubber dam on Falgu).</li>\n                <li><strong>Damodar River:</strong> Rises on the Palamu hills (Chotanagpur); traverses the industrial coal belt of Jharkhand and Bengal, joining the <strong>Hooghly River</strong> near Shyampur, south of Kolkata.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Deltaic Bifurcation: Farakka Barrage</h4>\n            <p>At <strong>Farakka</strong> in Murshidabad district of West Bengal, the Ganga reaches its deltaic apex and bifurcates:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Bhagirathi-Hooghly (Right distributary):</strong> Flows south through West Bengal past Kolkata into the Bay of Bengal at Sagar Island.</li>\n                <li><strong>Padma (Main branch):</strong> Flows southeast into Bangladesh, joined by the Jamuna (Brahmaputra) at Goalundo and the Meghna at Chandpur to form the world's largest delta.</p>\n        ",
