            "id": "m08",
            "title": "The Yamuna River System: Origin, Chambal Basin & Triveni Sangam",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Yamuna River</strong> is the longest and most important tributary of the Ganga. Extending over a course of <strong>$1,376\text{ km}$</strong>, it drains an extensive basin of $3.66\text{ lakh sq km}$ across Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh, Haryana, Delhi, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, and Uttar Pradesh.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Himalayan Headwaters & Mountain Course</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Source:</strong> Originates at <strong>Yamunotri Glacier</strong> at an elevation of $\\sim 6,387\text{ m}$ on the southwestern slopes of the <strong>Bandarpunch Peak</strong> in Uttarkashi district, Uttarakhand.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Tons Confluence:</strong> Near Dakpathar/Kalsi (Dehradun), the Yamuna receives its largest Himalayan tributary, the <strong>Tons River</strong> (originating from Har-ki-Dun valley). The Tons carries almost double the water volume of the infant Yamuna.</li>\n                <li><strong>Exit into Plains:</strong> Enters the plains near Tajewala/Hathnikund in Yamunanagar district (Haryana), where water is diverted into the Western Yamuna Canal and Eastern Yamuna Canal.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Plain Course & The Chambal System</h4>\n            <p>Flowing past <strong>Delhi</strong>, <strong>Mathura</strong>, and <strong>Agra</strong> (skirting the Taj Mahal), the Yamuna receives major right-bank tributaries draining the Peninsular shield:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Chambal River (~960 km):</strong> Originates at Janapav hills near Mhow on the northern slopes of the Vindhyas. Famous for its deep gorges, pristine unpolluted waters (protecting gharials, dolphins, and turtles in the National Chambal Sanctuary), and the four-dam cascade:\n                    <ol>\n                        <li><strong>Gandhi Sagar Dam (MP)</strong></li>\n                        <li><strong>Rana Pratap Sagar Dam (Rawatbhata, Rajasthan)</strong></li>\n                        <li><strong>Jawahar Sagar Dam (Rajasthan)</strong></li>\n                        <li><strong>Kota Barrage (Rajasthan)</strong></li>\n                    </ol>\n                    Joined by the <strong>Banas</strong> (from Aravallis), <strong>Kali Sindh</strong>, and <strong>Parbati</strong> before meeting the Yamuna near Etawah.\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Sind River:</strong> Rises in Vidisha (MP) and joins Yamuna in Jalaun.</li>\n                <li><strong>Betwa River (Vetravati, ~590 km):</strong> Originates near Kumra village in Raisen (MP). Flows past Sanchi and Orchha; site of the <strong>Matatila Dam</strong> and <strong>Rajghat Dam</strong>; meets Yamuna near Hamirpur.</li>\n                <li><strong>Ken River (Karnavati, ~427 km):</strong> Rises in Kaimur hills; traverses Panna National Park; joins Yamuna at Chilla near Banda.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. The Ken-Betwa River Interlinking Project</h4>\n            <p>India's flagship river-interlinking initiative transfers surplus water from the <strong>Ken River</strong> to the water-deficit <strong>Betwa basin</strong> via the Daudhan Dam and a 221 km link canal, securing irrigation and drinking water for the drought-prone <strong>Bundelkhand region</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>4. Triveni Sangam (Prayagraj)</h4>\n            <p>At <strong>Prayagraj (Allahabad)</strong>, the deep green-blue waters of the Yamuna merge with the turbid, silt-laden yellowish-grey current of the Ganga at the sacred <strong>Triveni Sangam</strong> (confluence of Ganga, Yamuna, and the mythical subterranean Saraswati), host of the historic Kumbh Mela.</p>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Yamuna is the LONGEST tributary of the Ganga (~1,376 km) and the second largest by discharge.",
                "Originates from the Yamunotri Glacier on the southwestern slopes of the Bandarpunch Peak (6,316 m) in Uttarkashi, Uttarakhand.",
                "Joined in the mountains by the Tons River (its largest Himalayan tributary, carrying more water than the Yamuna itself).",
                "Flows through Delhi, Mathura, and Agra, uniting with the Ganga at Prayagraj (Triveni Sangam).",
                "Major right-bank tributaries from the Central Highlands: Chambal, Sind, Betwa, and Ken."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "Chambal River rises near Mhow (Janapav hills, Vindhyan range) and flows through a gorge between Gandhi Sagar Dam (MP), Rana Pratap Sagar Dam, Jawahar Sagar Dam, and Kota Barrage (Rajasthan). The Ken-Betwa River Interlinking Project is India's first river link project under the National Perspective Plan, connecting the Ken (Panna) to the Betwa to irrigate Bundelkhand."
            ],
