            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Brahmaputra River System</strong> is one of the world's mightiest trans-boundary river networks, discharging more water than all European rivers combined. Spanning an estimated total length of <strong>$2,900\text{ to }3,848\text{ km}$</strong> (of which $916\text{ km}$ is in India), it drains an immense basin across Tibet (China), India, Bhutan, and Bangladesh.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Tibetan Source: The Yarlung Tsangpo</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Source Glaciers:</strong> Rises at an elevation of over $5,150\text{ m}$ from the <strong>Chemayungdung / Angsi Glacier</strong> in the Kailash range, just south of Lake Mansarovar in southwestern Tibet.</li>\n                <li><strong>Tibetan Course:</strong> Known in Tibet as the <strong>Yarlung Tsangpo</strong> (meaning <em>'The Purifier'</em>). It flows eastward across the dry Tibetan plateau for nearly $1,700\text{ km}$, paralleling the Great Himalayan crest along the tectonic <strong>Indus-Tsangpo Suture Zone (ITSZ)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Major Tibetan Tributaries:</strong> Receives the <em>Raka Zangbo</em>, the <em>Lhasa River (Kyi Chu)</em> on which the holy capital Lhasa is situated, and the <em>Nyang Chu</em> near Shigatse.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The Grand Syntaxial Bend & Canyon</h4>\n            <p>Upon reaching the eastern terminus of the Himalayas at <strong>Namcha Barwa (7,782 m)</strong>, the Tsangpo executes a breathtaking 180° hairpin turn. It carves the <strong>Yarlung Tsangpo Grand Canyon</strong>—recognized as the deepest canyon on Earth (maximum depth exceeding $5,300\text{ meters}$ and length of $504\text{ km}$)—dropping over $2,000\text{ meters}$ in elevation in a succession of cascades and cataracts.</p>\n\n            <h4>3. Entry into India: Siang and Dihang</h4>\n            <p>The river debouches into Arunachal Pradesh near Gelling, where it is known as the <strong>Siang</strong>. Descending through the gorges of the Abor Hills past Pasighat, it emerges onto the plain as the <strong>Dihang</strong>.</p>\n\n            <h4>4. Formation of the Master Brahmaputra at Sadiya</h4>\n            <p>Near <strong>Sadiya</strong> in upper Assam, the Dihang is joined simultaneously by two powerful mountain rivers on its left bank:</p>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Dibang (Sikang):</strong> Drains the Mishmi Hills of northeastern Arunachal Pradesh.</li>\n                <li><strong>Lohit:</strong> Originates in the Zayul region of eastern Tibet, flowing through the Mishmi crystalline ranges. The <strong>Dhola-Sadiya Bridge (Bhupen Hazarika Setu)</strong>, India's longest river bridge ($9.15\text{ km}$), spans the Lohit River.</li>\n            </ul>\n            <p>Downstream of this tri-confluence near Kobo, the united river formally acquires the proud Sanskrit title: <strong>The BRAHMAPUTRA ('Son of Brahma')</strong>.</p>\n        ",
