            "title": "The Brahmaputra System: Major Tributaries & Teesta River",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>Throughout its <strong>$720\text{ km}$</strong> course through the Assam Valley, the <strong>Brahmaputra</strong> absorbs dozens of large, fast-flowing tributaries descending from the high Eastern Himalayas to the north and the Indo-Burma ranges to the south.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Major Right-Bank (Himalayan) Tributaries</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Subansiri River:</strong> <strong>The largest tributary of the Brahmaputra.</strong> An antecedent river originating in the Mount Porom area of Tibet. Slices through the Arunachal Himalayas between the Miri and Abor hills; enters Assam in Dhemaji/Lakhimpur. Site of the <strong>Subansiri Lower Hydroelectric Project (2,000 MW)</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Kameng River (Jia Bhareli):</strong> Rises near the Indo-Tibet border in Tawang/West Kameng. Passes through the gorges between the Dafla Hills; flows past <strong>Pakke Tiger Reserve</strong> in Arunachal and <strong>Nameri National Park</strong> in Assam.</li>\n                <li><strong>Manas River:</strong> A major transboundary antecedent river rising in southern Tibet and draining western Bhutan. Traverses the pristine <strong>Manas National Park</strong> (UNESCO World Heritage Site and Biosphere Reserve) before joining the Brahmaputra at Jogighopa.</li>\n                <li><strong>Sankosh River (Puna Tsang Chu):</strong> Originates in northern Bhutan, forming the border between West Bengal and Assam before meeting the Brahmaputra.</li>\n                <li><strong>Teesta River:</strong> The lifeline of Sikkim and North Bengal. Originates from <strong>Tso Lhamo Lake (Cholamu Lake, 5,330 m)</strong> and the <strong>Zemu Glacier</strong> in North Sikkim. Cuts a magnificent gorge past Kalimpong and Jalpaiguri, entering Bangladesh to join the Jamuna (Brahmaputra).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. The 1787 Teesta Avulsion</h4>\n            <p>Prior to 1787, the Teesta flowed southward to join the <strong>Ganga (Padma)</strong> via the Atrai and Punarbhaba rivers. A catastrophic monsoon flood and earthquake in <strong>1787</strong> breached the natural sediment levees, violently diverting the entire course of the Teesta eastward into the <strong>Brahmaputra (Jamuna)</strong>—a classic case of large-scale river capture/avulsion.</p>\n\n            <h4>3. Major Left-Bank (Southern) Tributaries</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Burhi Dihing:</strong> Drains the Patkai Bum hills, traversing the historic oilfields of Digboi and Naharkatiya before meeting the Brahmaputra in Dibrugarh.</li>\n                <li><strong>Dhansiri:</strong> Originates from the Laisang peak of the Naga Hills; flows through Dimapur (Nagaland) and flanks the eastern boundary of Kaziranga National Park.</li>\n                <li><strong>Kopili:</strong> Rises in the Meghalaya Plateau (Jaintia Hills) and Karbi Anglong, joining the Brahmaputra in Morigaon district.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
