            "id": "m11",
            "title": "Evolution & Morphotectonics of the Peninsular Drainage",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Peninsular Drainage System</strong> represents an ancient, stabilized river regime that reached equilibrium hundreds of millions of years before the Himalayas rose out of the Tethys Sea. Its mature profile is evidenced by broad, shallow U-shaped valleys, low gradients, graded long-profiles, and the absence of rapid vertical downcutting.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Three Major Geological Catalysts of Peninsular Drainage</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>1. Subsidence of the Western Flank:</strong> During the Late Cretaceous and Early Tertiary (approx. 66–50 mya), as the Indian plate rifted away from Madagascar and the Seychelles, the western margin of the Peninsular Shield fractured and subsided beneath the Arabian Sea. This severed the western drainage networks and left the Western Ghats as an abrupt, steep fault escarpment, creating a master watershed situated remarkably close (50–80 km) to the western coast.</li>\n                <li><strong>2. Uplift of the Himalayas & Eastward Tilting:</strong> The intense compressional impact with the Eurasian plate caused the northern edge of the Peninsular block to warp downward into the Indo-Gangetic trough, while gently tilting the entire Peninsular craton towards the east and southeast. This subtle tilt permanently locked the course of major peninsular trunk rivers (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri) eastward into the Bay of Bengal.</li>\n                <li><strong>3. Tectonic Rifting of Narmada and Tapti:</strong> During the Deccan volcanic outpourings, deep-seated crustal lineaments (the <strong>Narmada-Son-Tapti Lineament</strong>) underwent normal down-faulting, creating linear fault grabens (rift valleys). Because these structural troughs sloped westward toward the subsided Arabian Sea margin, the Narmada and Tapti flowed westward, defying the general eastward tilt of the peninsula.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Hydrological Characteristics of Peninsular Streams</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Characteristic</th><th>Scientific Explanation / Consequence</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Rain-fed Regime</strong></td><td>Dependent entirely on monsoon rains; lack glacial meltwater, leading to severe winter/summer low flows.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Mature Valleys</strong></td><td>Gentle gradients ($<30\text{ cm/km}$); rivers have eroded close to their base-level of erosion.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Hard Rock Substrate</strong></td><td>Flow over resistant crystalline granites and basalts, preventing rapid bank meanders; channels are structurally fixed.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Dams & Reservoirs</strong></td><td>Require massive multi-purpose reservoirs (e.g., Nagarjuna Sagar, Srisailam, Hirakud) to store monsoon floods for year-round irrigation.</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Peninsular drainage system is vastly older than the Himalayan drainage, characterized by mature, senile, graded river valleys.",
                "Three major geological events shaped its present architecture: 1. Subsidence of the western flank during early Tertiary, 2. Upheaval of the Himalayas causing slight eastward tilting of the Peninsular block, 3. Faulting creating the Narmada-Tapti rift valleys.",
                "Peninsular rivers are non-perennial (rain-fed), experiencing low flows in summer and flash floods during monsoons.",
                "Broad, shallow valleys with low hydraulic gradients prevent deep vertical gorge cutting."
            ],
