            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Mahanadi River System</strong> is the master drainage basin of central-eastern India, extending over a length of <strong>$851\text{ km}$</strong> and draining a basin of approximately $1.41\text{ lakh sq km}$ across Chhattisgarh ($53\\%$), Odisha ($46\\%$), and marginal tracts of MP, Jharkhand, and Maharashtra.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Source, Course & The Chhattisgarh Plain</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Origin:</strong> Rises in a small pool at <strong>Sihawa (elevation ~442 m)</strong> in Dhamtari district of Chhattisgarh, on the northern foothills of the Bastar plateau.</li>\n                <li><strong>The Chhattisgarh Basin:</strong> Flows north and northeast through a synclinal structural basin underlain by Cuddapah limestones—the <strong>Chhattisgarh Plain</strong>. Famed as the <em>'Rice Bowl of Central India'</em>, it receives intensive irrigation from the Mahanadi and its tributaries.</li>\n                <li><strong>Entry into Odisha:</strong> Below its confluence with the Mand and Ib, it enters Odisha, traversing the Sambalpur basin and slicing through a deep $22\text{ km}$ long gorge across the Eastern Ghats at <strong>Satkosia Gorge</strong> (Satkosia Tiger Reserve).</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Systematic Profile of Mahanadi Tributaries</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Left-Bank Tributaries:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Seonath (Shivnath):</strong> The <strong>longest tributary of the Mahanadi (~380 km)</strong>, rising in Rajnandgaon and joining near Shibrinarayan.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Hasdeo:</strong> Rises in Koriya; flows past the Korba coalfield; site of the <em>Hasdeo Bango (Minimata) Dam</em>.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Mand:</strong> Drains the Raigarh coalfield and joins the Mahanadi near Chandrapur.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Ib:</strong> Rises in Jashpur; drains the Ib Valley coalfields into the Hirakud reservoir.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Right-Bank Tributaries:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Jonk:</strong> Rises in the Nawapara plateau.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Ong:</strong> Drains Bargarh district.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Tel:</strong> The <strong>most important right-bank tributary (~296 km)</strong>, rising in Nabarangpur and meeting the Mahanadi at Subarnapur (Sonepur).</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Hirakud Dam: The Earthen Colossus</h4>\n            <p>Constructed in <strong>1957</strong> near Sambalpur (Odisha), <strong>Hirakud Dam</strong> is one of the world's longest composite masonry and earthen dams. The main dam spans <strong>$4.8\text{ km}$</strong>, while the total system including flanking dykes extends over <strong>$25.8\text{ km}$</strong>, impounding the vast <em>Hirakud Reservoir</em> (a designated Ramsar site).</p>\n\n            <h4>4. The Brahmani and Baitarani Network</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Brahmani River (~799 km):</strong> Formed at <strong>Vedvyas near Rourkela</strong> by the confluence of the <strong>Sankh</strong> and the <strong>South Koel</strong>. Traverses the industrial belt of Angul-Talcher; site of the <strong>Rengali Dam</strong>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Baitarani River (~365 km):</strong> Rises in Gonasika in the Guptaganga hills of Keonjhar district.</li>\n                <li><strong>Dhamra Estuary & Bhitarkanika:</strong> Brahmani and Baitarani unite near Dhamra, forming the vast <strong>Bhitarkanika Mangroves</strong> (India's second largest mangrove ecosystem, famous for saltwater crocodiles).</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Mahanadi is the premier river of central-eastern India (~851 km long), draining Chhattisgarh and Odisha into the Bay of Bengal.",
                "Rises in the Sihawa highlands of Dhamtari district, Chhattisgarh; drains the fertile Chhattisgarh Plain ('Rice Bowl of Central India').",
                "Site of Hirakud Dam (Sambalpur, Odisha), one of the longest earthen dams in the world (main dam 4.8 km, total dykes ~25.8 km).",
                "Major Left-Bank Tributaries: Seonath (longest), Hasdeo, Mand, Ib; Major Right-Bank Tributaries: Ong, Tel, Jonk."
            ],
