            "title": "The Kaveri (Cauvery) System: Basin Dynamics & Inter-State Disputes",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>The <strong>Kaveri (Cauvery) River</strong> is celebrated as the <em>'Ganga of the South'</em> (Ponni in Tamil). Extending over a course of <strong>$800\text{ km}$</strong>, it drains an intensively cultivated basin of $81,155\text{ sq km}$ across Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Kerala, and the Union Territory of Puducherry.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Source & Hydrological Uniqueness</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Origin:</strong> Rises at <strong>Talakaveri</strong> in the Brahmagiri range of Kodagu (Coorg) district, Karnataka, at an altitude of $\\sim 1,341\text{ m}$.</li>\n                <li><strong>Perennial Regime (Dual Monsoon):</strong> Unlike the Godavari or Krishna, the Kaveri exhibits remarkably steady year-round flow. Its upper catchment in the Western Ghats (Kodagu, Wayanad) receives torrential rain during the <strong>Southwest Monsoon (June–September)</strong>, while its lower catchment and delta in Tamil Nadu receive peak rainfall from the <strong>Northeast Monsoon (October–December)</strong>.</li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>2. Systematic Profile of Major Tributaries</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Left-Bank Tributaries:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Harangi:</strong> Rises in the Pushpagiri hills of Kodagu.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Hemavati:</strong> Rises in the Western Ghats of Chikkamagaluru; site of the <em>Gorur Dam</em> in Hassan.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Shimsha:</strong> Rises in Tumakuru; site of an early hydroelectric station.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Arkavathi:</strong> Rises in Nandi Hills near Bengaluru; joined by the Suvarnamukhi.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n                <li><strong>Right-Bank Tributaries:</strong>\n                    <ul>\n                        <li><strong>Kabini (Kapila):</strong> Rises in the Wayanad hills of Kerala (formed by Panamaram and Mananthavady); flows past Nagarhole; site of Kabini reservoir (Karnataka).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Bhavani:</strong> Rises in the Silent Valley/Nilgiri hills (Kerala/TN); site of the colossal <em>Bhavanisagar Dam</em>; joins Kaveri at Bhavani (Kooduthurai).</li>\n                        <li><strong>Noyyal:</strong> Rises in Vellingiri hills; passes Coimbatore and Tirupur.</li>\n                        <li><strong>Amaravati:</strong> Rises in the Anaimalai hills; meets Kaveri at Karur.</li>\n                    </ul>\n                </li>\n            </ul>\n\n            <h4>3. Three Sacred Riverine Islands & Waterfalls</h4>\n            <p>Along its course, the Kaveri splits into two branches and reunites three times, creating three celebrated sacred islands housing temples of Lord Ranganatha (Vishnu):</p>\n            <ol>\n                <li><strong>Adi Ranga (Srirangapatna, Karnataka):</strong> Historic capital of Tipu Sultan.</li>\n                <li><strong>Madhya Ranga (Shivanasamudra, Karnataka):</strong> The river cascades down the twin waterfalls of <strong>Gaganachukki and Bharachukki</strong> (drop 98 m). Here, the historic <strong>Shivanasamudra Hydroelectric Plant</strong> was commissioned in <strong>1902</strong> to supply electricity to the Kolar Gold Fields (KGF)—Asia's first commercial hydel station.</li>\n                <li><strong>Antya Ranga (Srirangam, Tamil Nadu):</strong> The largest functioning Hindu temple complex in the world, situated between the Kaveri and its northern distributary, the <strong>Kollidam (Coleroon)</strong>.</li>\n            </ol>\n\n            <h4>4. Major Dams & Inter-State Water Sharing</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Krishna Raja Sagara (KRS) Dam:</strong> Built in 1932 by Sir M. Visvesvaraya across the Kaveri below its confluence with the Hemavati and Lakshmantirtha in Mandya (Karnataka). Adjacent to the famous <em>Brindavan Gardens</em>.</li>\n                <li><strong>Mettur Dam (Stanley Reservoir):</strong> Built in 1934 across a gorge in Salem district, Tamil Nadu; the primary storage dam regulating irrigation into the Kaveri Delta.</li>\n                <li><strong>Grand Anicut (Kallanai):</strong> Built by Chola king Karikalan in the 2nd century CE—one of the oldest water-diversion structures in the world still in use.</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Kaveri is the sacred river of southern India (~800 km long), draining Karnataka (42%), Tamil Nadu (54%), and Kerala (4%).",
                "Originates at Talakaveri in the Brahmagiri Range of Kodagu (Coorg) district, Karnataka, at ~1,341 m elevation.",
                "Characterized by less seasonal fluctuation than other peninsular rivers because its upper basin receives Southwest Monsoon (summer) while its lower basin receives Northeast Monsoon (winter).",
                "Forms three sacred riverine islands: Srirangapatna (Karnataka), Shivanasamudra (Karnataka), and Srirangam (Tamil Nadu).",
                "Shivanasamudra Falls (drop 98 m) hosted India's first major hydroelectric station (1902)."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "Major Left-Bank Tributaries: Harangi, Hemavati (Gorur dam), Shimsha, and Arkavathi. Major Right-Bank Tributaries: Lakshmantirtha, Kabini, Suvarnavathi, Bhavani (Bhavanisagar dam), Noyyal, and Amaravati. Major dams include Krishna Raja Sagara (KRS) Dam in Mysuru/Mandya (Karnataka) and Mettur Dam (Stanley Reservoir) in Salem (Tamil Nadu). The Cauvery Water Disputes Tribunal (CWDT) and Supreme Court (2018) allocated water shares between TN, Karnataka, Kerala, and Puducherry."
            ],
