            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>India hosts an extraordinary array of <strong>natural and artificial lakes</strong> reflecting diverse geological origins—from tectonic fault basins and meteorite impact craters to glacial cirque tarns and coastal brackish lagoons.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Genetic Classification of Indian Lakes</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Genetic Type</th><th>Formation Mechanism</th><th>Prominent Indian Examples</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Tectonic Lakes</strong></td><td>Formed by crustal warping, faulting, or subsidence.</td><td><strong>Wular Lake</strong> (J&K—largest freshwater lake in India); <strong>Pangong Tso</strong> (Ladakh).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Impact / Crater Lakes</strong></td><td>Formed by hyper-velocity meteorite impact or volcanic explosion.</td><td><strong>Lonar Lake</strong> (Buldhana, Maharashtra—meteorite crater in basalt).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Glacial Lakes (Tarns)</strong></td><td>Formed by glacial gouging and moraine damming in high mountains.</td><td><strong>Roopkund</strong> ('Skeleton Lake', Chamoli); <strong>Chorabari Tal</strong> (Kedarnath); <strong>Tso Lhamo</strong> (Sikkim, ~5,330 m).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Coastal Lagoons</strong></td><td>Enclosed behind offshore sandspits and barrier beaches.</td><td><strong>Chilika Lake</strong> (Odisha—largest brackish lagoon); <strong>Pulicat Lake</strong> (AP/TN); <strong>Vembanad Kayal</strong> (Kerala).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Fluvial (Ox-bow) Lakes</strong></td><td>Abandoned river meander cut-offs during flood avulsion.</td><td><strong>Kanwar Lake (Kabartal)</strong> in Begusarai (Bihar); <strong>Deepor Beel</strong> (Guwahati, Assam).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Inland Saline Basins</strong></td><td>Centripetal desert depressions where evaporation concentrates salts.</td><td><strong>Sambhar Lake</strong> (Rajasthan—largest inland saline lake); Didwana; Pachpadra.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Lacustrine Wetland</strong></td><td>Tectonic depression with floating vegetation (phumdis).</td><td><strong>Loktak Lake</strong> (Manipur—Keibul Lamjao National Park).</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>2. Major Man-Made Reservoirs of India</h4>\n            <ul>\n                <li><strong>Govind Ballabh Pant Sagar (Rihand Dam, UP):</strong> India's largest artificial reservoir by volume ($12.2\text{ bcm}$).</li>\n                <li><strong>Indira Sagar (MP):</strong> Largest reservoir capacity in central India.</li>\n                <li><strong>Gobind Sagar (Bhakra Dam, Himachal Pradesh):</strong> Impounded by Bhakra Dam on the Satluj.</li>\n                <li><strong>Dhebar Lake (Jaisamand Lake, Udaipur, Rajasthan):</strong> Built in 1685 by Maharana Jai Singh; one of the oldest and largest historic artificial lakes in Asia (~87 sq km).</li>\n            </ul>\n        ",
