            "theoryHtml": "\n            <p>India's dramatic structural relief—ranging from the faulted western escarpment of the Sahyadris to the uplifted Horst of the Meghalaya Plateau and the step scarps of the Peninsular cratons—creates some of the most spectacular <strong>waterfalls and knickpoints</strong> in the world.</p>\n\n            <h4>1. Premier Waterfalls of India</h4>\n            <table class=\"table table-bordered\">\n                <thead>\n                    <tr><th>Waterfall</th><th>Height / Drop</th><th>River</th><th>State / Location</th><th>Geomorphic Significance</th></tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Kunchikal Falls</strong></td><td><strong>455 m</strong></td><td>Varahi River</td><td>Shivamogga, Karnataka</td><td><strong>Highest waterfall in India</strong> (multi-tiered cascade down Western Ghats).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Barehipani Falls</strong></td><td>399 m</td><td>Budhabalanga</td><td>Simlipal, Mayurbhanj, Odisha</td><td>Second highest waterfall; 2-tiered drop in Simlipal Tiger Reserve.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Nohkalikai Falls</strong></td><td><strong>340 m</strong></td><td>Rain-fed stream</td><td>East Khasi Hills, Meghalaya</td><td><strong>Tallest plunge waterfall in India</strong> (single uninterrupted free-fall drop off Sohra scarp).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Nohsngithiang Falls</strong><br>(Seven Sisters Falls)</td><td>315 m</td><td>Rain-fed stream</td><td>Cherrapunji, Meghalaya</td><td>Spectacular 7-segmented curtain cascading off the limestone scarp.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Dudhsagar Falls</strong></td><td>310 m</td><td>Mandovi River</td><td>Goa-Karnataka border</td><td>Four-tiered cascade; railway bridge passes directly across the fall face.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Jog / Gersoppa Falls</strong></td><td><strong>253 m</strong></td><td>Sharavati River</td><td>Shivamogga, Karnataka</td><td><strong>Tallest untiered single vertical drop plunge</strong> in India (Raja, Roarer, Rocket, Rani).</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Chitrakote Falls</strong></td><td>29 m (width 300 m)</td><td>Indravati River</td><td>Bastar, Chhattisgarh</td><td><strong>'Niagara of India'</strong> due to massive horseshoe-shaped monsoonal crest.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Shivanasamudra</strong></td><td>98 m</td><td>Kaveri River</td><td>Mandya/Chamarajanagar, Karnataka</td><td>Twin cataracts (Gaganachukki and Bharachukki); site of 1902 hydel station.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Hundru Falls</strong></td><td>98 m</td><td>Subarnarekha River</td><td>Ranchi, Jharkhand</td><td>Classic river rejuvenation knickpoint on the edge of the Ranchi Plateau.</td></tr>\n                    <tr><td><strong>Dhuandhar Falls</strong></td><td>30 m</td><td>Narmada River</td><td>Bhedaghat, Jabalpur, MP</td><td>'Smoke cascade' through the Marble Rocks gorge.</td></tr>\n                </tbody>\n            </table>\n\n            <h4>2. Geomorphic Formation of Knickpoints</h4>\n            <p>Waterfalls in Peninsular India act as <strong>knickpoints</strong>—sharp breaks in the longitudinal slope of a river profile. When the Peninsular block experienced Tertiary uplift or coastal down-faulting, rivers rejuvenated their erosive energy, carving steep vertical cataracts where they plunge from ancient crystalline upland plateaus onto lower coastal plains or rift valleys.</p>\n        ",
